for the past year we've looked at apples  based 13-inch MacBook Pro as a pretty  bad value compared to Apple's newly  updated MacBook Air and the more  expensive $1,800 touched by a pro since  it packs a pretty slow dual-core  processor and was lacking a few features  but with Apple's new $1300 base MacBook  Pro we've completely changed our mind  and we're now calling it the best value  macbook ever let's discuss everything  that's great and not so great about the  new base model MacBook Pro for starters  Apple is able to equip it with a touch  bar at the same base price something you  previously had to pay eighteen hundred  dollars to get now of course not  everybody likes it but what really  matters is the hardware that comes with  it like touch ID authentication which  makes it really convenient to log in and  install new apps without having to  re-enter your password but the major  deal is Apple's t2 chip which takes care  of a bunch of system controllers and  makes it much more secure and on top of  that it brings Hardware HEV C video  transcoding and many people believe that  HEV C is going to be the future of  mainstream codec so having the two-two  chip makes this MacBook Pro much more  future-proof design wise almost  everything else about the new base  macbook pro basically matches the more  expensive MacBook Pros like the very  color accurate and bright display with  true tone technology the class-leading  force touch trackpad in the all-aluminum  lightweight and incredibly thin design  along with that we also have the latest  version of Apple's butterfly keyboard  similar to the third gen in 2018 but  with updated materials this alone was a  big reason why some people would spend  more for the 2018  $1,800 model or go with a MacBook Air  instead the previous base macbook pro  had a keyboard design from 2016 which  was the most prone to failure now we're  not sure if the latest design will fix  all the problems just yet but if any  issues arise Apple will fix them for  free what really makes this year's base  MacBook Pro stand out is the new quad  core processor and from the first glance  on Apple's website it seems like it's  quite a bit slower than the $1,800  MacBook Pros 2.4 gigahertz processor but  that couldn't be further from the truth  we made a full video comparing both  models and we found that under load the  base MacBook Pro ran only 300 megahertz  slower than the $1,800 model nowhere  near the one gigahertz or a thousand  megahertz difference we saw on the spec  sheet we ran  ton of benchmarks video editing tests  and even some photo editing and found  that the $1,800 MacBook Pro is only  around five to nine percent faster than  the $1,300 model so for performance  alone  it definitely wasn't worth the upgrade  if you're looking to do video editing on  a budget we're able to playback full  resolution 4k h.264 and h.265 files  without any dropped frames and both  Premiere Pro and Final Cut Pro and even  adding color correction in a lot would  be perfectly fine using Final Cut while  Premiere would require you to drop down  a half resolution which is still very  usable but that's not the case for  editing in DaVinci Resolve which likes  to have more video memory like on the 15  inch with dedicated graphics overall  we're impressed with how this MacBook  Pro performs but keep in mind that final  exports will require some patience  compared to the 15-inch MacBook Pros  about two to three times longer than  your project length and Final Cut Pro or  up to 10 times longer in Premiere so  personally we'd stick with Final Cut for  most people that do simple 4k editing  from time to time this new MacBook Pro  will do a surprisingly good job  absolutely smoking the previous model  thanks to the quad-core CPU and updated  graphics the same thing goes for photo  editing but here the performance  difference between the 13 inch and 15  inch models are even lower  since graphics are hardly used importing  50 42 megapixel RAW images into  Lightroom classic was very quick and  exporting edited JPEGs was also really  impressive we did notice a small delay  in some tasks that we don't get on our  13-inch MacBook Pro with 16 gigs of ram  which makes sense since the 8 gigabytes  of RAM are maxed out while editing  photos upgrading to 16 gigs of ram will  not only smooth out your editing  experience but speed up exporting as  well if you're doing 12 to 24 megapixel  images and small batches 8 gigabytes  will perform well enough but if you're  working with higher resolution files or  doing things like HDR panorama are  working with hundreds of images we would  highly recommend spending $200 more and  upgrading to 16 gigabytes of RAM we also  decided to include some programming  tests but unfortunately us being video  editors with absolutely no Xcode  experience at all we weren't able to  find and run a sample Xcode project that  would be easy to replicate on future  machines so if you guys know of a sample  Xcode project we could use comment below  but from  what we've seen so far with this machine  you're getting truly impressive  processor performance for the cash out  of this 1.4 gigahertz CPU and we think  it's gonna perform much better than its  windows rivals and completely blow the  2019 MacBook Air out of the water well  be getting their hands on that soon and  doing a full comparison so make sure to  subscribe so you see that and now let's  talk about the downsides when we first  got this laptop we decided to take off  the bottom cover and compare the  internals to the $1,800 MacBook Pro and  we found a few interesting details first  off we're only getting a single fan in  the base model compared to two on the  more expensive pro but seeing as we got  such impressive performance results with  CPU temps that were only slightly hotter  than the $1,800 model that doesn't seem  to be a problem we also noticed that the  speaker's look way different and when we  tested them we noticed a huge  improvement on the more expensive  13-inch Pro just listen for yourself  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  and then we tested the SSD storage speed  and found that the 128 gigabyte SSD was  quite a bit slower than the 256 gig on  the $1,800 model and not only that but  if you paid the extra two hundred  dollars to get the 256 gig SSD it's  actually still slower than the 256 gig  on the $1,800 Pro so there are a couple  of downsides already but the biggest  difference is that the $1,300 model only  gets two Thunderbolt three ports while  the more expensive one gets four of them  so we can see that while the new base  macbook pro offers incredible value for  performance there are a few things that  you have to sacrifice but let's take a  look at all these things from my own  personal opinion and past experiences as  far as performance goes I think this  machine is the way to go if you don't do  professional work everyday or if you're  doing entry-level basic stuff like  editing YouTube videos or if you're  doing your work every single day or if  your business depends on it like editing  wedding videos then I would honestly  recommend just jumping to the 15-inch  model with dedicated graphics or if  you're doing a lot of photo editing or  programming I think just getting 16 gigs  of RAM on this model would be a great  choice for almost everyone and for the  single fan we didn't really notice  higher temps at all one difference we  noticed between this and the dual fan  model was that the single fan would keep  blowing at max fan speed during longer  tasks like exporting while the dual fan  would eventually slow down a bit after  temps came down but as far as  performance itself the single fan is  completely fine now for the speakers I  personally like to watch YouTube videos  shows and movies on my macbook so that's  actually the most important thing for  myself personally now if you prefer to  use headphones and you like to watch  youtube videos on your phone and shows  on your TV then the speaker difference  won't really matter that much to you and  in reality the $1,300 models speakers  are still better than practically any  Windows laptop out there asked for the  SSD speed difference even the slower  speeds of the 128 gig model are nearly  as fast as a lot of the premium laptops  out there like Dells XPS so it'll be  fine for most people so you should  really  just worry about the storage amount  itself unless you know you need faster  speeds and finally I think the factory  eight gates of RAM would be okay for a  lot of people but I personally love to  keep a lot of chrome tabs open so if  you're like me you'll be much better off  with 16 gigs of ram and especially if  you're doing productivity tasks more  than once or twice a week for the ports  I found that I don't really use them  that often  but even then it's really convenient to  be able to swap the charger to the other  side so it's a lot more convenient and I  also like to charge my phone using a USB  c2 lightening cable so that's already  maxing out two ports so if I needed to  plug something in and transfer some  files I need to unplug one of them on  the $1,300 model so if you're planning  on actively using the Thunderbolt 3  ports like for connecting an external  display or workin off on an external  drive you probably be better off going  for the $1,800 model with 4 ports now  you could make it work with a  Thunderbolt 3 hub off Amazon but it's  not as convenient to carry that around  but if you're a simple person who only  needs those ports for charging and  occasionally connecting an external  drive for some quick file transfers that  you should honestly just be fine with  those two ports one last question we  want to address that some of you may be  asking is if it's worth buying a  discount at 2018 for port model instead  of a lower and 2019 model now the 2018  versions perform almost as good as the  2019 in terms of CPU but they do have  keyboards that have an older design  personally if you were gonna buy a  $1,500 256 gig 2019 MacBook Pro and can  score an older 4 thunderbolt model for  up to $100 more we would make that jump  since you also get better speakers and  faster SSDs but only if you can get 16  gigs of RAM on that 2018 model all in  all if you want to get access to mac OS  and not have to worry about performance  issues in the future I think the 2019  based MacBook Pro for $1,300 is the best  value MacBook in years and I think  almost everyone will be happy with that  if you enjoyed this review make sure to  tap that like button and click the  circle above to subscribe so you don't  miss out on more videos like this one  thanks for watching and we'll see you in  the next video  [Music]  [Music]

